      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 123,
      "outputTokens": 909,
      "costUsd": 0.008242500000000002,
      "pronunciationText": "Start outside the bracket. In y equals two cos x, every height is multiplied by two: a swell twice as big. Peaks at one rise to two, troughs fall to minus two, and the points on the axis stay put, because two times zero is still zero. This is a vertical dilation by a factor of two. The amplitude, the distance from the middle line up to a peak, is now two. What if the factor is minus two? Every height changes sign, so the curve flips over the x-axis. The amplitude is still two, because a distance can't be negative."
    },
