      "pronunciationText": "The same rules work for tan x, with two differences. Tan x repeats every pi, not two pi, so in y equals tan two x, the period is pi divided by two, pi over two. The asymptotes, the vertical lines the curve never meets, move with the squash, to pi over four, three pi over four, and so on, every pi over two. And tan has no highest or lowest value. Its range is all real numbers, so it has no amplitude. A number in front still stretches it vertically, by that dilation factor, but it's not called an amplitude."
